// @test: run-interpreter
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: stdlib-runtime, list, references, value-slot
// @expected-stdout-begin
// 7 7 9
// eq true
// unique 3
// @expected-stdout-end

// `&*list.ptr.offset(i)` is a FOURTH producer of a returned `&T`, and it
// had been given the ADDRESS convention where the element is owed.
// docs/architecture/returned-reference-contract.md names the other three
// and the two conventions.
//
// Six lines said it:
//
//     let xs: List<Int> = [7, 7, 9];
//     print(f"{*a} {*b} {*c}")   ->  48624037456 48624037464 48624037472
//
// Three consecutive addresses, eight bytes apart, where 7 7 9 is stored.
// Two separate faults compose into that number and BOTH are needed to
// read it:
//
//   * `List.ptr` handed compiled code the backing ALLOCATION, whose
//     first 24 bytes are an ObjectHeader — so `offset(0)` addressed the
//     header's type_id word and `offset(3)` addressed element 0. Text
//     had the convention right all along (`alloc_text` stores the BYTES),
//     which is what made the disagreement visible.
//   * `*item` on the resulting reference lowered to the generic `Deref`,
//     whose Int arm is identity — so the address came straight back out.
//
// HOW IT WAS FOUND: `dedup()` kept only the first element of any Int
// input while `List.unique([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])` kept all five — two archive
// bodies disagreeing with each other AND with the truth. `List.contains`
// has the same `*item == *value` spelling and is correct, because it is
// one of the 41 of 96 `self.ptr` methods the interpreter INTERCEPTS, so
// its body never runs. The interceptions were hiding the size of this.
//
// The third line is here because the first two could pass while the
// stdlib caller still failed: `unique` is the caller that sits on this,
// and it needs `item.clone()` to reach the element too — which is why
// the reference carries the element rather than an address that `*`
// alone knows how to read.

fn main() {
    let xs: List<Int> = [7, 7, 9];
    let a: &Int = unsafe { &*xs.ptr.offset(0) };
    let b: &Int = unsafe { &*xs.ptr.offset(1) };
    let c: &Int = unsafe { &*xs.ptr.offset(2) };
    print(f"{*a} {*b} {*c}");
    print(f"eq {*a == *b}");
    print(f"unique {[1, 1, 2, 3, 3].unique().len()}");
}
